# #1272: a builtin type reference in a value position that supplies no signature. Every one of
# these constructs — the regression fixture next door pins that — so the refusal is SPY0342 ("no
# signature is available to pin"), never SPY0346 ("cannot be constructed"), which would be false
# and would send a reader to fix the wrong thing.
#
# The second half of #1272 landed: object, decimal, frozenset and frozendict now HAVE
# families, so `mk: () -> frozenset[int] = frozenset` pins the way `list` does. Unpinned, they draw
# the FAMILIED wording — an overload set, or generic arguments unknown here — with an annotation
# that would work. Iterator and DictKeyView keep the unfamilied wording: their registry entries are
# `typeof(object)` placeholders, so a family would emit `new object()` (#1346); `bytes` keeps it
# too, because a `Sharpy.Builtins.Bytes` overload set shadows the TYPE in a static-member
# qualifier (`bytes.fromhex(...)` -> CS0119) — #1347.
def main():
    # A plain binding, one per leak shape: CS0119 for the non-generic ones...
    a = object
    b = bytes
    c = decimal

    # ...and CS0305 for the generic ones, which reached codegen without type arguments.
    d = frozenset
    e = frozendict
    f = Iterator
    g = DictKeyView

    # An EXCEPTION type is a builtin with no family too, and refusing it as a value is the same
    # improvement. Its call-argument position is deliberately NOT refused — `assertRaises(ValueError,
    # ...)` is established working behavior, and re-typing it is the #1170 over-fire. The refusal is
    # gated on value uses only for this family, unlike the non-constructible one next door, so
    # `map(bytes, ns)` still leaks and stays under #1272's open half.
    h = ValueError
